From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] take cameras, frames, stills and RAW from V4L2 camera nodes

A device whose cameras are the V4L2 nodes of the host's camera device (ro.vendor.camera.config=v4l2)
gets its camera list, its characteristics, its frames and its controls from those nodes. Each camera
has a capture node and a metadata node. The capture node's description control and its ENUM_FMT,
ENUM_FRAMESIZES and ENUM_FRAMEINTERVALS calls say what the camera is and which sizes and rates it
runs; the HAL turns them into LIMITED static metadata on a template (emu_camera_v4l2.json, the
emulated back camera without the features this device does not offer), with the capabilities the
description earns: MANUAL_SENSOR and READ_SENSOR_SETTINGS for a single lens and RAW once calibrated.
A camera that states RAW also states the lens shading map modes OFF and ON, as the device info
requires of every RAW capable camera (the template's OFF alone made it reject a calibrated lens at
boot, and the camera was gone from the list), the hot pixel map modes OFF and ON with the map among
its result keys, and that its RAW data has no lens shading correction applied
(android.sensor.info.lensShadingApplied FALSE: the host copies the platform's Bayer pixels and the
HAL's shading map is all ones). A result for the hot pixel map mode ON carries an empty map, since
the host hands over no list of defective pixels. A camera without RAW keeps the template's values.
BURST_CAPTURE is never declared, because the host cannot meet its conditions, and the maximum latency
is unknown.

The sizes are the host's own plus the ones the camera API requires of every camera (1920x1080,
1280x720, 640x480 and 320x240 as JPEG, those that fit the largest video size as YUV and PRIVATE, and
1920x1440 with 1280x720 beside them for a camera that reaches that size). A size the host does not
serve is cut from the smallest native NV12 mode that covers it, scaled with libyuv and, for a JPEG,
encoded by the HAL's own compressor with the Exif data of the stock sensor. The Exif thumbnail of
such a picture is cropped to its aspect ratio before it is scaled, never stretched.

The frame source queues the framework's gralloc buffer as a dma-buf where the node can import it
(its chroma may start where the node's image puts it, padding included) and copies from the node's
own buffer where it cannot, with the reason logged once per stream. It writes the request's controls
before the first frame, and a control is retired only when the node says it does not exist, never for
a refused value. A request has one deadline; a wait that ends without its buffer takes the stream's
buffers back, so the rest of the request fails at once and a late frame cannot land in memory the
framework has reused. The exposure's values (exposure, gain, white balance, focus, 3A states, the
neutral color point and the noise profile of a RAW) come from the metadata node's record, matched by
sequence.

Each video output of a request has its own host stream, so that one exposure fills a preview and a
recording of one size (on one stream they took an exposure each, which halved the frame rate), and
the stream of an output that stays configured but leaves the requests (a recording that stopped)
closes after half a second and two requests that took no still, so that the platform's restart for
the largest open size comes then; an output that leaves the configuration closes with the
reconfiguration, which makes a new frame source. A request that takes a still counts for nothing:
a still holds the pipeline for as long as the host takes to make the picture, and the requests of
the preview wait behind it, behind one still or a queue of them, so that preview keeps its stream
and the host its capture session; the absences that a video output earned before such a request
start again from zero at it, so that a queue of mixed captures (two YUV, then two JPEG) does not
close the preview that waits behind it; a stream that the request about to begin uses is never closed
(camera_streams.hpp). With auto-exposure on, the camera runs at the top of the requested frame rate
range, not at its midpoint. A JPEG made here from a frame that the device made black (blocked,
interrupted, resuming) fails, as a native still does. The Exif exposure time and aperture are
written only for a positive, finite value. The Exif focal length in 35 mm film follows from the
camera's stated focal length and physical sensor size, which the host's description states as a
pair (the field of view's own equivalent): the V4L2 frame source hands them to the Exif writer, a
camera whose description states none gets no tag, and the template's default size does not pass for
a stated one; the sensor characteristics take both from the static metadata for any other camera.

Stills are encoded by the host with a gap for the Exif data: the HAL builds the Exif data and a
thumbnail from the result and writes them into the gap in place, then the blob header at the end of
the buffer. RAW16 is copied from the still node. Discovery opens only /dev/video10 and above, where
the driver registers the camera's nodes; the codec nodes are below.

The source reports a request whose outputs all failed (the host refused them, or the deadline
passed) as failed as a whole, by the rules of camera_request.hpp, which also holds the request's
deadline: 3 seconds, or 15 with a still (the host gives a started still 10 and the rest of its group
2 more).

